00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.640
Hello, everyone.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.480
My name is Jacqueline
Coley and I

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.360
am delighted to moderate this
conversation with The Academy's

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.520
Visiting Artist series.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.080
The person we're
speaking with today

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:38.560
has an incredible, and
enviable, filmography.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.000
So, without further ado, please
allow me to welcome our guest

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.040
today, Sanaa Hamri.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.360
How are you, ma'am?

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.840
I'm good, I'm good.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.600
Thanks for having me.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.280
That clip was such a great
way to start this conversation

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.440
because it took me back
to watching this film--

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.800
which I saw in the theater--

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.840
because that trailer
just hooked me in the way

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.840
it did the first time I saw it.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.160
I don't know what trailer I
saw it for but I was like,

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.760
yes I need to see this film.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.720
It has all of my
absolute favorite people

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.600
and it's just an
incredible thing.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.440
What is it like watching
that trailer again?

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.880
Did it feel like you're
going back in time?

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.080
It's interesting.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.520
I definitely felt like I
was going back in time,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.400
but I felt like it held up
with what is going on today.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.600
And I love the fact
that the movie still--

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.160
Something New-- still holds up.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.240
And I love all those
actors, and I was just

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.160
thinking how amazing
they did and how

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.000
it's just a fun, fun movie.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.280
Yeah, no, it's one of, I think,
several incredible romantic and

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.880
friendship-driven movies that
are part of your filmography.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.600
And we're definitely
going to discuss them

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.840
as we talk about your
career and your work.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.920
But we're going to
kind of take it back

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.720
because, as I said
in my introduction,

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.760
you have an enviable
filmography.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.720
And it started
from the beginning

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.680
of being enviable because
you started literally

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.600
with Queen Mariah.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.280
Directing her in your very first
credit, which, let's just say,

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.160
by that point I would have
given up and like, this is it.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.160
There's nothing going
to top directing Mariah.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:20.720
So talk about starting
off in music videos, how

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.040
you got that first credit, which
I know so many folks watching

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:28.320
this can think back to or
maybe of thinking towards.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.760
Well, I started
off as an editor.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:36.000
And I was editing a lot
of the cheaper rap videos,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.600
like hip hop videos.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.080
And then I had a small
career of just learning

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.560
a lot about music.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.080
And it was through
kind of the eyes

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:48.920
of the rappers of the time.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.360
And I met Mariah as
an editor because one

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.800
of the editors, the really
famous, go-to editor

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.960
was not available.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.800
So I wound up having
to step in and edit

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.040
for Hype Williams at the time.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.400
And she walked in
the editing room

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:10.480
and she had never
seen a woman of color.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.200
You know, at that
time, I was way young.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.640
And usually it was a
certain type of person

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.760
who would be behind
that Avid machine.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.960
So she was very
curious about me.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.800
I knew a little bit
about her music.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.160
But I grew up in
Morocco, so I wasn't as

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.000
familiar with certain things.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.920
So you know, we slowly
got to know each other

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.200
through the editing bay.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.720
And it was her idea
for me to direct.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.400
And I really wasn't.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.440
I was like, I went
to theater school.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.200
I went to Sarah Lawrence and
studied theater and psychology.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.000
So I never went to film school.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.800
I'm self-taught as an editor.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.280
And when she suggested
that I should direct

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.160
one of her videos, I
literally was like, Mariah,

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.360
I don't want to
screw up the video.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.840
I've never directed before.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.400
And she's like, Sanaa,
I see your vision.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.400
I see your eye.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.760
And she was like, I've worked
with a lot of directors

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.680
and I know you can do this.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:05.520
So I really trusted her.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.480
And I did my first
video with her,

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.680
which was the Thank God I Found
You remix video with Joe &amp; Nas.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.040
And it became like
the number one video.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.000
And back in the day, they
would put our names on it.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.440
So people were like, yo,
you're a music video director.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:22.760
And I'm like, oh, I don't know.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.160
I was like, I'm still an editor.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:25.560
It was a one-off.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.400
And then a lot of the other
artists started calling me,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.360
like Destiny's Child,
Jay-Z. And they're like,

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:32.160
can you do a video?

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:33.200
We know you're an editor.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.120
But could you do a video for us?

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.880
And I'm like, OK,
I'll do one more.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.040
And then, all of a sudden, I
became a music video director.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.160
And I still edited all my stuff.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.240
Nobody called me
anymore for editing jobs

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.720
because I was competing with the
directors that were hiring me.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.120
And so I learned a lot
through Mariah's eyes, well,

00:06:54.120 --> 00:07:00.800
she is very astute to what's
a great looking shot, fashion,

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.480
all of that world that I
actually never knew about.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.000
I knew more about
Moliere and Shakespeare

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.120
versus who is the hottest
fashion designer at that time.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.080
So I kind got to learn that.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:17.480
And then she took
me on tour with her.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.680
And you know,
directing tours, it's

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.080
a whole other kind of
thing in the wheelhouse.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.560
And I mean, I just
really-- it was boot camp.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.120
I really learned a
lot about filmmaking.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.480
Because when you're working
with an artist like Mariah,

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.520
you're using really high
level cinematographers.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.880
So I literally was a
student of these DPs

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.760
in learning about lighting, and
just what with the great lens

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:45.440
is.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.680
So I've been very fortunate
in terms of my schooling

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.320
as a filmmaker there with her.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.520
And I love hearing
you talk about her.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.760
As someone who's just
been a fan of her music

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.720
and obviously just
seeing her from afar,

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.560
I love how she saw something
in another female artist.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:07.640
And with the position that
she's in, she's Mariah Carey.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:09.000
If she says, this
is the person I

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.360
want to direct the
music video, you're

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.040
the person directing
the music video.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.920
And I think that is
so essential, I think,

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.960
when we talk about how
Hollywood is going to change.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.000
It's her seeing you in that
edit bay which then just keeps

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.760
that thing going to where
young filmmakers or just people

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.960
like myself who wanted
to write about movies,

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.960
seeing a movie like
yours and being like,

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.480
this is the type of
movie that I want

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.040
to talk about and write about.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:38.280
And I love how that's the
cyclical fashion of filmmaking.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:40.800
But Mariah wasn't the first,
and she wasn't the last,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:43.000
I would say, in your just
incredibly storied music

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:43.480
career.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.720
But music videos,
it's interesting

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:46.640
that you mention that.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.680
Because I think it breeds
incredible independent film

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.960
directors because you are
on very tight timelines.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:56.520
You are usually with
not budgets that

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.360
are as friendly as
the visuals they

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.160
would like to see upon them.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:01.240
And so I'm just curious.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:02.640
You said that it
was a boot camp.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.440
What is something that you
learned during that time

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.560
that you specifically still
think about when you're

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.200
approaching some of
your features like Just

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.000
Right or Sisterhood of
the Traveling Pants 2?

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.880
I think the music
video realm really

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:22.520
teaches you how to work
under pressure and quickly.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:26.040
It is kind of synonymous
with filmmaking, I would say.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.640
Because a lot of
times, with budgets

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.400
and in all of those
things, you have

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.520
to be able to think
really quick on your feet

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:38.680
and give a really great product.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.560
And I think the
music video realm,

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:44.480
you know, we get booked on
jobs like three days before.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:49.800
You get a call and it's like,
Jay-Z wants to film wherever.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:50.680
Are you available?

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.400
Or Puffy, or whoever
it is at the time.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:54.840
And you have to
think quick and you

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.400
have to deliver the product.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.240
So I feel like
that kind of hones

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:03.160
you for that energy that
you bring to the table

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:05.040
when you're filming.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:08.120
And also, I solve
a lot of problems

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:10.480
that are creative
problems when I'm on set.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:12.880
I can look at something and
then all of a sudden I'm like,

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.000
oh, this is not working.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:18.200
Like, I know it's not working
and I just immediately pivot.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.480
Like, no fear is the key.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.240
You have to be able to
look at something, say, no,

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:25.280
that's not going to work.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:26.240
I'm pivoting.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.280
We're going to do it this
way, and do it in the time

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:31.960
so that you make your
days and all that.

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:35.560
So I definitely
find the music video

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:38.280
training is guerrilla style.

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:42.720
And you're also dealing
with the music industry.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:44.280
And how can I say this?

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.800
But the music
industry is a mishmash

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.440
of people that come
from all walks of life.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:53.400
And you can deal with
people who are lawyers

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.640
down to drug dealers.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:58.600
So you have to really be
able to handle people.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:02.000
And entourages, and
artists' entourages,

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.800
and artists feeling
a certain way.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:07.400
So I think it's such
a heightened melting

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:13.040
pot of the best of what could
go in filmmaking and the worst.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:13.520
Yeah.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.560
I think that it's a
crucible, like you said.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.160
And what comes out of it is
these incredible filmmakers.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.040
And I especially
love when I speak

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.120
to filmmakers, or artists,
or creatives like yourself

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.680
that come from
different backgrounds.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:30.720
One of my favorite film
composers, you know,

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.320
he's a classically
trained pianist.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:35.720
But he also was very much in
depth into hip-hop culture

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:37.680
and was in an
instrumental hip hop band.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.040
I'm talking about the Oscar
nominated composer Nicholas

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:41.240
Britell.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:45.200
And just seeing him and
how he works with Pusher

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:49.760
to do a remix for the stuff
that he does with succession.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:53.200
And I think those sort of
different, I would say,

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.600
influences really bring into it.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.200
So I'm just curious.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:59.240
Obviously, theater
is a medium where

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:00.680
there's a lot of crossover.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:03.520
But back to your
Sarah Lawrence days,

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.200
I think there's just definitely
something about your time there

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:09.200
that is something that is
not maybe necessarily seen

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:11.240
in a lot of folks that
are in a director's chair

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:13.000
that you could sort
of meld into that.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.120
Did you see that
when you were first,

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.320
I would say, directing
music videos,

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.560
but more definitely
with the features?

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.360
Well, I think when I was
on the set of Something New

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:25.720
and I was finally directing
actors with dialogue,

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:29.000
I realized what that Sarah
Lawrence College training

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.280
was in theater brought to me.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:32.920
Because I was able to--

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.880
I was trained in Alexander
Technique and all of the stuff

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.880
that actors have to do to
open up their instrument, make

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:40.080
them feel comfortable.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:43.240
And I was not really using it
when I was doing music videos

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:44.400
and commercials.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.280
I only finally was able to
use it in my first feature.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:52.440
And right on the set
of Something New,

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:57.600
I realized this is where
I'm supposed to be.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.800
The music video thing felt
very transitory and more

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:03.880
like I was going to grad school.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:06.840
[LAUGHS] And then, on the
set of Something New I said,

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:10.680
oh, I'm a full
fledged film director.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:15.400
And I always felt like
I had a lot to say.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.600
And that's why the music
video format for me

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.520
was kind of stifling
because I wanted

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:24.360
to tell so much of a story and
always have a point of view.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:27.400
What I consciously did before
I did my first film was I

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:31.320
only did music videos where
I felt like had a purpose.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.760
Here and there I did a video,
like I did a Nicki Minaj video

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:36.600
or whatever.

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:41.080
But mainly, I wanted
to uplift the vision

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:46.440
of people of color and women
in music video pop culture.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:47.720
And I figured I had a voice.

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:50.400
So I was doing that.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:54.600
But then I felt like I needed
to do that in a larger format

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.560
to be able to
really tell a story.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.440
So, Something New.

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:02.520
At that point, I go, OK,
this is where I'm headed.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:03.960
And I have a lot to say.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:08.840
And I and I want to always
bring a point of view.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.640
Entertain, but at
the same time not

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:15.600
have caricatureish
cliched characters

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.640
that have been perpetuated
for a long time.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:20.920
So those things matter to me.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:21.960
And you did it.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.280
You're correct on your
very first feature.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:26.480
And it's so funny that you
say that your music video

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.040
days and that time in commercial
work was like grad school.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.960
Because I'm like, if
your grad school includes

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.680
Prince, and Jay-Z, and Christina
Aguilera, and Mariah Carey,

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:36.080
that's the grad school
I would like to go to.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.600
But let's talk about
that graduation process.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.640
Because as I talk to
a lot of filmmakers

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:45.480
who have moved from commercial
work or music videos,

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:47.720
there is a bit of
a glass ceiling

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:50.200
there where, although
you have this

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.920
maybe I would say storied
career within the medium,

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:54.920
obviously you know
how to direct.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:58.160
But getting someone
to say, hey, we're

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:01.440
going to trust you to sort of
jump into this next level is

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.080
sometimes very
difficult for folks

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:07.240
to get that level of trust
from producers, or actors,

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:07.800
or everyone.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:09.840
So how did the script--

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.600
I mean, how did Something New,
the whole process come to you?

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:15.640
And then, that cast as well.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.160
Because that is
just an incredible

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:19.760
cast to have on your
very first feature.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.600
I mean, Sanaa Lathan,
which, first of all,

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.160
give it up for
everyone on that crew,

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:24.960
making sure they get
your two names correct.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:26.400
[LAUGHS] And then also--

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:26.960
It was rough.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:27.840
--Simon Baker.

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:28.560
I know.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:29.400
It was rough.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.080
We kept turning every time.

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:33.920
I said, I'm going
to call you Kenya.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:34.800
You're Kenya on set.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:35.600
I'm Sanaa.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:37.360
And she's just, I got it.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:38.120
I got it.

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:39.560
Kenya, Sanaa.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:41.200
I still think people
would mix that up.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:47.720
And Simon, and Donald, and Alfre
Woodard, and Blair Underwood.

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:51.000
But yeah, talk about taking that
step to your very first feature

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.920
film, which was incredible.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:53.960
Incredible film.

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:57.880
Well, just like the sisterhood
that Mariah and I had--

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:01.400
and it takes real,
true sisterhood--

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:05.040
the other sisterhood
was Stephanie Allain

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:11.320
who produced Something New
who took a chance on me.

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:14.360
And at that time,
if you think of it,

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.800
people like me were
not around like now.

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:20.320
Now, everybody, thankfully.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:21.720
Way overdue.

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:24.360
But I mean, I would
walk into rooms

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:27.320
and I mean, it
would really ruffle

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:32.880
feathers just the way I looked,
the fact that I was a female,

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:35.640
that I was mixed,
that they didn't know

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.320
what, they didn't know how.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.640
I mean, all of that stuff,
I'm very conscious of it.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.680
And I'm confident in who I am.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:45.000
I have to be.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.600
But it took a sisterhood.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:50.280
And it took Stephanie Allain.

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:51.360
We had a conversation.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.320
I didn't even know how
to meet on a script.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:55.240
Like, I read the script
and I really loved it,

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:59.360
but I had some notes.

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:02.800
And I talked to
Stephanie about it.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:07.920
And I said, look, I
really want to add this.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:10.880
The scene with the
hair with them in bed

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:16.000
was something that I kind of
thought up and told the writer,

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.120
I want us to tackle
those things.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:19.960
I know it's
uncomfortable for us,

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:24.120
but we need to talk about
these things, especially--

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:26.160
He's like, well,
do you sew it in?

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.560
And making Kenya feeling
uncomfortable about

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:30.320
that she has a
sew-in, and all that.

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:31.800
Boy, back in the
day, people weren't

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:33.120
talking about that stuff.

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:35.360
So it took that.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:39.200
It's important that
women and women of color

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.480
take the leap of
faith with each other.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:44.200
And that's what it took.

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:47.320
See, had Mariah not
said I could direct,

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:50.320
and Stephanie Allain didn't
give me my first feature.

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:52.200
And of course,
Focus approved it.

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:59.000
But Stephanie, she made sure
it happened, and she got me.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.680
And that's how I was able
to kind of jump in it.

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:05.000
It wasn't an easy
road after that,

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.240
let's be clear,
because we're still

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:08.880
dealing with all that stuff.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:12.120
But it's way better now.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.160
Everything is as it should be.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:15.640
That's how I view life.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:17.560
So I'm not in resistance.

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:22.280
I'm always continuing
excellence, and continuing

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:26.800
the legacy of women,
of people from Africa,

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:29.840
and all women of color
all over the world.

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:32.480
So that's how I
view those things.

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:35.080
So that was kind of like that
jumping into something new.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:38.200
And doing my first
feature felt really good.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.320
The actors were so
respectful and great.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:44.840
They actually knew my music
video work at that time.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:46.840
So I wasn't like
an unknown entity,

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:49.040
it just was my first feature.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:53.680
And you know, I
learned a lot on it.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:56.400
But I also felt comfortable.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.960
I knew that was my calling.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.080
That to me really mattered.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.640
And I loved working
with a diverse group

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:09.360
of people with different
point of views, different all

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:10.120
of that.

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:15.840
And I actually made the
characters- Sometimes

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.480
one of the characters, I don't
know it was Donald Faison.

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:19.920
But somebody was
like, man, are we

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:21.520
going too hard on the white boy?

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:22.760
I was like, no.

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.480
I was like, we have to.

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:25.200
I was like, no.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:27.080
If you've been in an
interracial relationship,

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:28.600
you know that it is not hard.

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:29.880
He got about right.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.360
And then Mike Epps was like, yo.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:35.440
I remember Mike
pulling me aside.

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:38.720
He said, I think I'm going
to be alienating my fans.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:40.640
I was like, we don't
care about that, Mike.

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:42.080
I was like, we have to do this.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:43.200
He goes, all right.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:44.680
He goes, Sanaa, all right.

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:48.440
And back then, I
mean, you couldn't.

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:50.120
And I was like, keep
going, because they

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:51.480
improved a lot of this stuff.

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:54.000
And I think it's a fun movie.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:55.200
We're not trying to--

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:59.880
but it kind of
helped frame a future

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:03.520
because I believe
that images are things

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:05.720
and it does sway the future.

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:08.080
And I want to sway it
in the good direction.

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:10.440
So to me it's like
that was that piece

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:16.920
that helped sway us for
self-acceptance and being open

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:17.600
minded.

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:19.040
Because the fact
of the matter is,

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:23.240
I was Kenya's character--
not as open minded.

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:26.160
I hadn't dated a white boy.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:28.200
That's what I related to.

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:32.680
And I came up with all the
reasons why I felt that.

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:35.880
And then Blair
Underwood character

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:40.560
was the brother who was
actually a good guy,

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:45.800
but he wanted a
certain type of woman.

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:47.000
He didn't see her.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:48.600
For folks that haven't
seen the film--

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:49.800
He didn't see her.

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:51.920
Sanaa Lathan's character
goes on a bit of a journey

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:56.200
to not just embrace who she
is by having her natural hair,

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:57.880
but by saying who she is.

00:20:57.880 --> 00:20:59.680
She's a very high
powered Black woman,

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:02.360
which is actually
something people

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:03.480
are talking about more now.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:05.600
Black women are one of
the most high, as far as

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:07.640
per capita, at high
levels of education,

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:09.400
high levels of achievement.

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:11.960
And then the dating
situation around that

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:14.320
is quite precarious,
particularly

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:16.320
when certain people
judge you a certain way

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:19.360
if you date somebody
outside of your race.

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:22.040
And this film not only
brought a light to that,

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:23.800
but tackled it in
a way that was just

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:25.560
filled with such authenticity.

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:28.840
And I think there were so
many moments within that.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:31.640
But I was just normalizing
the difference between women

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:34.920
wearing a sew-in versus
wearing their natural hair,

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:37.960
normalizing the conversations
that you would actually have

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:41.040
with your female friends if this
was the first person outside

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:43.440
of your race that
you were dating,

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:46.600
normalizing being a Black
woman in corporate America

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:50.320
and realizing the double
standards of that existence--

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:52.600
the twice as hard
to get half as much.

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:55.000
All of these things are things
that folks talk about now.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:59.440
But this was not something that
folks were seeing on TV shows.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:01.640
And I think you can make
a direct correlation

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:06.400
between seeing Issa Rae wearing
a head wrap to bed in Insecure

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.560
and some of the stuff
that you guys talked

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:11.600
about with Something
New, which is incredible.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:13.840
But we're not going to just
talk about Something New

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:17.720
because obviously this is just
the beginning of what you said.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:20.480
But you touched
on something, too.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.680
Sundance had this
incredible statistic

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:28.320
that Barry said a few
years ago that they had,

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.360
I think it was 50%
of the films that

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:31.800
were at the Sundance
Film Festival

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:33.280
were directed by women.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:36.280
But the years between
a woman debuting

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:37.960
at Sundance and
her second feature,

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:40.080
or any debut from
a female director

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:42.400
and their second feature
is sometimes years.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.440
And sometimes it's
the second movie

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:47.960
that's the hardest one
to get, in a lot of ways.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:50.240
And so, talk about
that journey for you.

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:52.600
Because I can guess based
on just this conversation,

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:54.200
but also just based
on what I know

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:57.160
talking to female filmmakers,
that was another battle--

00:22:57.160 --> 00:23:00.800
another moment to sort
of prove that you're

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:03.120
going to continue on this path.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:03.600
Right.

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:08.640
So obviously you think when
you do your first film--

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.360
which is usually the case
for the men who do do that--

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:16.280
they wind up like, if it was
a guy who did Something New,

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:18.120
he would have done
Batman, the next one.

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:20.200
Like, it goes automatically
and really quick.

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:21.200
That's just the truth.

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:22.920
I'm not mad at him.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:25.120
I want everybody
to be successful.

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:27.920
But I'm just talking math.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:32.800
So it's clearly always
an uphill battle.

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:38.920
Only until recently this
past year that you feel,

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:40.920
oh, like, wow.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.000
Like, it's shifted,
which is, thank God.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:47.200
But I was always used
to that uphill battle.

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:50.080
And I always say, it's the
survival of the fittest.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:52.280
It's not a sprint,
it's a marathon.

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:58.600
So I have that kind of fighter
mentality ingrained in me.

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:02.760
So getting to that
second feature

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:04.560
was really, really tough.

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:07.080
Because of course,
when they see you,

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:09.040
which is really
interesting process

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:12.960
that what you have on the
outside and your agenda

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:17.400
is going to determine if you're
capable of doing something

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:19.400
when clearly, we're
all very capable.

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:21.880
So it took a long time.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:26.920
I wasn't up for a lot of movies.

00:24:26.920 --> 00:24:30.360
But then let's talk about
the other Sisterhood again.

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:31.040
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:32.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:34.160
For as long as I could
remember, the four of us

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:35.120
shared everything.

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:36.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:40.040
(SINGING) You were
always playing hard.

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:43.080
I believed that the sisterhood
could survive anything.

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:43.600
[GIGGLES]

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:46.360
(SINGING) That voice within.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:49.680
But we had to learn on our
own how to become ourselves

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:51.200
without losing each other.

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:51.680
[YELPS]

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:57.360
(SINGING) Don't look
away, don't run away.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:58.280
Baby--

00:24:58.280 --> 00:24:58.800
Whoa!

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:00.720
(SINGING) --it's only life.

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:04.920
So the other sisterhood
is Debra Martin Chase,

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:08.120
who was the producer
of Traveling Pants.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:10.840
So now we're starting
to see this correlation

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:13.040
in every single opportunity.

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:15.960
It was the Mariahs,
the Stephanie Allains,

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:18.560
and the Debra Martin
Chases of the world.

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:23.920
So Debra was the one who really
pushed them to consider me.

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:26.840
And Alcon was a really
great place for me, too,

00:25:26.840 --> 00:25:30.280
because they're kind of
nontraditional and independent

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:31.240
guys.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:33.160
You got Andrew and Broderick.

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:35.240
Broderick's Black,
Andrew's white.

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:38.480
You know, so it's a
different kind of vibe.

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:42.120
Debra really championed
me for the second film.

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:44.800
And then I wound up doing
a third movie with Debra.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:46.600
And I think that really
made a difference,

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:50.320
is when you have a producer
who looks at you as a filmmaker

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:56.840
and doesn't genderize you
and doesn't color you.

00:25:56.840 --> 00:26:02.960
Because I bring my
experience, my growing

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:06.200
up in Africa, all of those
things comes with it.

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:11.080
But at the same time, I
want the same opportunities.

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:14.560
And we should all give each
other the same opportunities.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:21.760
I feel like Debra was really
key in continuing that motion.

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:24.720
And it wasn't easy then, too.

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:28.640
Even doing the movie wasn't
easy, and all of that stuff.

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:31.880
So after that film, too,
it was really difficult.

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:34.360
I was still dabbling.

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:37.480
While I was doing those movies,
I was still dabbling in music,

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:39.000
I have to be real.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:40.480
I was working a lot with Prince.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:41.280
I was his creative director.

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:42.240
That's what I was just going to.

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:43.000
[LAUGHS]

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.920
I was this creative
director for like, a decade.

00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:51.000
And I kept working
with him on the side.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.760
He was like my side gig.

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:57.160
And he would always say, man,
are you moonlighting again?

00:26:57.160 --> 00:26:57.960
And I'm like, yeah.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.800
I was like, I can't
do it this weekend,

00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:01.120
but I could do it next weekend.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:03.640
So literally, he was
my side gig while I

00:27:03.640 --> 00:27:06.240
was trying to pursue this.

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:10.200
Prince was very--
he was with me.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:14.000
He came with me as my guest to
the premiere of Something New.

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.520
And that was when he
first saw the film.

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:19.440
And he was like, it's
ahead of its time.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:21.000
And then he was
very encouraging.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:22.680
He knew what the battle was.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:25.840
Remember, he did direct a movie
and he's been around Hollywood.

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:27.520
He knows what it takes.

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:37.000
 
 
636
00:27:20,690 --&gt; 00:27:27,140
I think he was there to
encourage me and keep me going.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:38.200
Like, yeah, you can do this.

00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:39.600
But at the same
time, I could tell

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:46.320
at times he would feel for
me and know how the road kept

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:49.080
getting steeper and steeper
when it should have not

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:50.800
gotten steeper and steeper.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:55.880
But again, I was born
and raised in Africa.

00:27:55.880 --> 00:27:59.480
And when you have that
kind of upbringing,

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:04.560
an entire continent of I
look at as like my ancestors.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:06.160
I was like, I am not giving up.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:07.840
Like, I'm going to get there.

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:09.040
It can get as steep as--

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:12.080
I'll start climbing up
like this, whatever it is.

00:28:12.080 --> 00:28:15.040
But you know, I've
had the great support.

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:17.840
So that was happening while
I was doing the other movie.

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:20.680
So what was good is I
didn't have to take work

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:22.920
that I didn't want to do.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:27.640
And during that time,
I was interested in TV.

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:30.880
And I was like, man, I
want to have my own show.

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:33.000
So I was like, let me try
directing here and there.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:36.560
Let me learn about this TV
industry, which was completely

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:40.720
different from film, completely
different from music videos,

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:43.840
different breed of
people, different culture,

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:45.240
different way
people do business.

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:46.920
I was like, man,
they have lunches,

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:48.160
they get their weekends off.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:49.120
I was like, oh.

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:50.760
I was like, I've
got to get into--

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:52.520
Like literally,
because I was used

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:55.320
to filming on the
weekends a music video,

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:58.160
doing movies, and
then going on tour.

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:02.240
And then I said, wow, these TV
people, they have a good life.

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:04.120
[LAUGHS] So I just--

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:05.280
It's a good gig.

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:06.160
Good gig.

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:07.760
Good gig and content.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:10.920
So I kind of went because
I mean, I'm a creator.

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:14.720
So I want to have my
own shows, and again

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:19.440
to have characters
that are authentic,

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:21.720
that represent the now.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:28.200
Female characters not
being pawns and active

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:30.680
at the service of
male characters.

00:29:30.680 --> 00:29:34.200
People of color being in
positions that we all are in,

00:29:34.200 --> 00:29:36.920
and then seeing that and
seeing the trials, tribulations

00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:37.480
of that.

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:41.360
So I feel like the meet--
entertainment in any form

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:47.000
is where we can make
a change in the world.

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:50.160
And that's all that matters
to me in anything that I do.

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:52.200
That's why I move
between mediums

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:53.680
because I'm like,
oh, if I can't,

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:55.320
let me try to reach this group.

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:57.160
Let me try to reach
the younger group.

00:29:57.160 --> 00:30:00.120
I always try to stay in
the ear of the youth.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:07.320
Gen Z, I study them so that I
can keep pushing that needle.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:08.360
That's incredible.

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:11.120
And I wish more people
approached it the way you do,

00:30:11.120 --> 00:30:13.600
as a student of, really, people.

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:14.800
Of sociology, really.

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:16.480
I mean, that's really
what you're doing.

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:18.560
And that I think
is why when I look

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:21.240
at so many of the characters
that you've directed,

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:23.880
from Queen Latifah's
character in Just Wright,

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:26.040
to the girls in Sisterhood
of the Traveling Pants,

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:28.200
to some of the stuff
you did with Empire

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:32.160
and some of the other shows,
they feel like real people.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:33.640
They do not feel
like caricatures.

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:36.480
Even when they're doing these
very sort of broad things,

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:38.400
it still feels like
this is somebody

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:41.760
that I would know from around
the way, especially for me

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:43.880
when we get to Just Wright.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:47.400
But I wanted to touch on real
quick with the Prince stuff

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:50.360
because he also seemed
to me to be, as you said,

00:30:50.360 --> 00:30:56.640
sort of an artist's advocate and
just an advocate for creatives

00:30:56.640 --> 00:31:00.200
being given the freedom to just
do, to just create, to just

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:00.960
make art.

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:03.560
And he always just
seemed to me anyone

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:05.560
who got a chance
to, like you did,

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:11.040
work with him intensely and sort
of learn at the side of him,

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:13.240
and also help him
craft his vision.

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:16.080
They all talk about
how just stunned

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:18.360
they were with his talent,
and the talent that

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.040
maybe even he was stunned by.

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:21.760
But more, they were
stunned by the fact

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:24.640
that he wanted to rise
everyone around him.

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:26.880
And I think you've already
talked on it a little bit.

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:30.720
But I just would imagine that
was another not an education,

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:33.680
but a different
sort of perspective

00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:36.600
to how you could approach
filmmaking after that, too.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:37.520
Absolutely.

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:40.880
I mean, Mariah gave me
the base, but he also

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:44.160
gave me the point of view.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:45.840
And it was another school.

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:47.120
I went through his school.

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:50.160
And it's kind of like the
school of James Brown.

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:54.400
I mean, it's a different type
of [LAUGHS] a different type.

00:31:54.400 --> 00:31:57.080
Basically, I was always
inherently musical.

00:31:57.080 --> 00:31:59.440
Remember, I used to
edit images to music.

00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:03.200
So I inherently have
music behind me.

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:09.840
He really got me up to his level
because I would edit for him,

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:11.480
I would film him.

00:32:11.480 --> 00:32:14.960
When he would do
concerts and stuff,

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:17.200
rehearsal, all
the lighting cues.

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:20.240
You see, he knew about lighting
cues like nobody else did.

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:23.040
And I literally learned all
about the lighting cues.

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:24.320
He's like, was that late?

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:26.560
And meanwhile, it's
like a microsecond late.

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:28.040
And I'm like, yeah,
I think it was.

00:32:28.040 --> 00:32:29.720
He goes, well, it can't be late.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:32.000
So I would have to
time some of the cues.

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:37.760
So Prince gave me this
manual, tactile, sonic way

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:39.000
of approaching things.

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:44.680
So after going through his
school, I can hear everything.

00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:48.960
And anything that's out of sync,
off beat or whatever, somebody

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:53.520
claps off-beat like you
see that, or dance off

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:56.720
beat or whatever, I catch
it before most people,

00:32:56.720 --> 00:32:58.600
and it's because of him.

00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:01.800
And we film so much
that a lot of stuff

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:04.920
is not out or
anything like that.

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:07.240
Like, I spent a lot of time
in the studio with him,

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:10.040
and Maceo Parker, and a
lot of different artists.

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:13.760
And being around
that live music,

00:33:13.760 --> 00:33:16.320
and seeing it being
recorded, and just being

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:20.000
around that process
really taught me

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:21.640
a lot about filmmaking.

00:33:21.640 --> 00:33:26.680
And it opened my mind visually
of what one can do, what works.

00:33:26.680 --> 00:33:29.640
Seeing an image and
what does that sound.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:32.600
So score is so important to me.

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:34.720
And he was so good
at making score.

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:36.680
He made a few pieces for me.

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:38.200
He was like, well,
what do you want?

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:39.760
I'm like, I want to
kind of like this.

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:42.080
And he's like, well, I'm
not making elevator music,

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:43.800
he'll say.

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:47.480
[LAUGHS] I was like, I
don't want elevator music.

00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:48.360
But we don't need it.

00:33:48.360 --> 00:33:50.000
Like, they're
eating in the cafe.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:54.520
Like, we can't have a guitar
solo here, it's distracting.

00:33:54.520 --> 00:34:00.360
But it was really good,
really good school.

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:05.160
And I'm so grateful for that.

00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:09.280
Sound is part of filmmaking
and so important.

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:13.480
And the tones of sound
really can indicate a scene.

00:34:13.480 --> 00:34:17.920
And I really learned a
lot from Prince on that.

00:34:17.920 --> 00:34:20.320
I should have known a music
video director directed

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:23.840
Something New when you
open with that love song

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:27.800
because I literally bopped
to that song for, like, weeks

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:28.840
after I saw that.

00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:30.480
And this was back
when you couldn't just

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:35.160
go to Spotify or Shazam.

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:37.840
I had to go back and be
like, what is this song?

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:40.440
I'm like, watching the credits
to pick it out of the credits

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:42.480
so I can go buy it later.

00:34:42.480 --> 00:34:43.960
Again, tell a little bit my age.

00:34:43.960 --> 00:34:45.840
But yeah, no.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:49.320
And all of your movies, both
music cues and as well as

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:51.920
scoring, it drives things.

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:54.000
I think about the basketball
scenes in Just Wright

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:55.840
and how you guys set
up stuff with that.

00:34:55.840 --> 00:34:57.720
It's the same thing.

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:01.240
We do have also some questions
from the students that

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:02.920
had submitted for you here.

00:35:02.920 --> 00:35:05.000
And they definitely want
to definitely talk to you

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:06.440
about craft and technicality.

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:08.320
But before we get
there, I want to kind of

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:10.160
bring it to a full circle
moment because you're right.

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:12.200
You've done a ton of
incredible television.

00:35:12.200 --> 00:35:14.680
But I thought the
television show obviously

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:16.760
that felt like such a
great full circle moment

00:35:16.760 --> 00:35:21.680
is you directing on Empire
because I feel like it marries

00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:24.040
the roots of your
filmmaking and even

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:25.560
the roots of your
very first feature

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:29.040
because you're reteaming with
Taraji who, if folks don't

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:30.960
know, her quote in
the trailer which

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:33.080
is like, I'm going to give
them black, ashy babies.

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.640
That is like, she's just so
incredible in that movie.

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:39.520
And I can imagine it
was kind of interesting

00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:40.520
coming back to this now.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:42.200
I mean, this is her show.

00:35:42.200 --> 00:35:44.920
And coming back in to
direct, again, I just

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:47.480
feel like it had to be felt
like a bow on everything

00:35:47.480 --> 00:35:49.240
that you've done thus far.

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:50.080
Absolutely.

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:54.480
When I was approached
with Empire

00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:59.600
I was approached by Lee Daniels,
and Danny Strong, and Ilene

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:00.240
Chaiken.

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:04.200
And Lee hadn't done
television before.

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:07.640
And Lee Daniels
and I were talking.

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:09.520
And he was like, we
need somebody like you

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:11.840
because you've done a little
TV, but you get the vision

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:12.800
of the music industry.

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:15.000
And you'll be my ears and eyes.

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:17.280
And I was like, I love it.

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:18.320
I want to do it.

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:23.480
And it was my first
executive producer job,

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:25.360
and I directed a bunch.

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:28.680
But that was kind of like
my next phase in life

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:32.480
as being an executive
producer and understanding

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:34.720
how to do that in
television, which was

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:36.280
another part of the boot camp.

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:40.200
And seeing Taraji there and
kind of collaborating with her

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:41.600
on a different level was great.

00:36:41.600 --> 00:36:43.960
Because Taraji, when I
put her in Something New,

00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:45.400
she had just started.

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:47.720
And Stephanie Allain was the
one who told me about her.

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:50.760
And I looked at her tape
and I was like, I like her.

00:36:50.760 --> 00:36:51.520
Let's put her in.

00:36:51.520 --> 00:36:52.840
I like her authenticity.

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:55.440
So then, now she's
Cookie on Empire

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:58.880
and we spent so
much time together.

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:02.520
And that was almost
seven years of my life.

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:06.120
So I kind of disappeared up
in Chicago where we filmed it.

00:37:06.120 --> 00:37:10.640
And you know, it was a really
good kind of full circle

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:14.040
moment for me because it took
place in the music industry,

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:14.760
which I knew.

00:37:14.760 --> 00:37:17.560
It had a lot of drama and
music, it had fashion.

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:20.160
It had all of those elements
that I was in charge

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:22.520
of from the visual sense.

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:25.440
We had writers in LA, but
everything else was me.

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:30.240
And a great testament
to Lee Daniels and Danny

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:34.800
Strong for them trusting
me with continuing

00:37:34.800 --> 00:37:41.520
the vision and kind of making
it my own thing as well.

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:42.240
I love that.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:44.200
And yeah, when I was--

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:45.840
I obviously knew
some of your work.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:48.640
But when I clocked on how
much you did with Empire,

00:37:48.640 --> 00:37:52.920
I was like, you don't know the
job that you're essentially

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:55.600
learning how to do
when you get there.

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:58.080
But I feel like every
stop along the way

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.080
before Empire was
training you how

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:04.640
to do that incredible job that
we all saw for all those weeks

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:07.400
on television, like how
to do that at that level.

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:10.600
Because if folks don't
remember, Empire was a thing.

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:12.360
It was like, when
that came out I,

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:14.360
remember everybody's
like, you watching Cookie?

00:38:14.360 --> 00:38:15.280
Are you watching this?

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:16.720
It was like it was such there.

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:20.040
And it has to have people
like you, and Lee, and Taraji,

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:23.000
and Terrence involved to
put that into the DNA of it.

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:24.600
And you can absolutely see it.

00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:25.120
Absolutely.

00:38:25.120 --> 00:38:29.040
And we were all film
people, which is great.

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:31.200
I learned a lot about
the TV industry.

00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:36.320
But it really took film people,
that chemistry between us

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:40.720
and being like really excellent
at everything, and down

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:43.720
to the production design.

00:38:43.720 --> 00:38:47.240
I mean, we were
matching wallpaper

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:51.280
to curtains we were in it
like a movie on every episode.

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:52.800
And it was a lot.

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:55.200
It was a lot to carry,
but well worth it.

00:38:55.200 --> 00:39:01.400
Because I think Empire opened
up for a lot more content,

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:04.560
whether it was
movies or television,

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:06.520
with a diverse group of people.

00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:08.000
How about that?

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:08.960
Yeah.

00:39:08.960 --> 00:39:09.480
That looked--

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:10.320
Telling very authentic stories.

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:10.920
--like the world.

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:11.400
Yeah.

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:12.440
Not white-washed stories.

00:39:12.440 --> 00:39:14.320
Completely within the culture.

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:15.320
Totally.

00:39:15.320 --> 00:39:19.080
And you know, we have to have
those avenues because the world

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:21.240
doesn't look like one thing.

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:24.880
It has many faces that
should all be included.

00:39:24.880 --> 00:39:27.600
So I'm very proud of that show.

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:29.760
I especially like
the part where you

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:31.960
talked about the
collaborative process of it.

00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:33.360
And you've been
great in the sense

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:35.400
that you talk about
letting people go wrong.

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:37.800
But also-- and this is another
question from the folks

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:40.680
here-- is, sometimes you have
to push back as the person who's

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:42.480
got the 10,000 feet view.

00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:44.880
And sometimes that's
with executives.

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:48.360
And so this question
is, as a director,

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:50.720
how do you go about getting
the approvals from studio

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:52.880
executives maybe
when they're saying,

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:53.920
we don't want to do this.

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:56.280
Like, how do you
thoughtfully push back?

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:59.320
Because I think that is as
big a part of the job as it

00:39:59.320 --> 00:40:00.520
is to see the vision.

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:02.480
Because if you can't get
people to buy into it,

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:03.600
it's never going to happen.

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:05.760
OK, so this is where I start.

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:08.960
First, I'm not doing
a project with people

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:11.200
that don't mutually
kind of share

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:15.440
that end result.
So I always say,

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:19.120
when you place yourself in that
position, you're going to lose.

00:40:19.120 --> 00:40:23.760
Because if you're thinking about
apples, and they want oranges,

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:25.320
and you're just
trying to get the gig

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:27.840
and say, OK, I'm going to do
an orange, then you get the gig

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:30.840
and then you're like, I'm doing
apples, then you're in a fight.

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:34.120
So I don't place myself
in those positions.

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:39.120
The studio, the execs
are my partners.

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:43.160
And when I go in, I look
to them to be partners.

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:46.120
And I'm very clear before
I even start the project,

00:40:46.120 --> 00:40:48.200
this is what I'm going for.

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:50.160
And I do visual decks and stuff.

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:51.640
And then I hear their feedback.

00:40:51.640 --> 00:40:53.040
And there could
be little things.

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:54.600
That kind of like,
oh, you know what?

00:40:54.600 --> 00:40:56.840
We didn't see this
character like this.

00:40:56.840 --> 00:40:58.160
She seems a little bit--

00:40:58.160 --> 00:40:59.720
I'll ask questions.

00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:01.680
Like, why do you feel that way?

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:02.320
What is it?

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:05.080
A lot of times,
the execs, it could

00:41:05.080 --> 00:41:10.120
be a small tweak for me to do
but it sounds big for them.

00:41:10.120 --> 00:41:14.080
And I think it's about
really being a good listener

00:41:14.080 --> 00:41:15.000
and collaborative.

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:17.080
But if you just
start the project

00:41:17.080 --> 00:41:20.320
as they're your partners, you're
already winning half the time.

00:41:20.320 --> 00:41:22.360
So then it's like
the little things.

00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:26.080
There are times in which
choices that are made they're

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:27.240
not understanding.

00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:29.680
I've run into that.

00:41:29.680 --> 00:41:35.480
I've run into a situation in
which a studio exec did not

00:41:35.480 --> 00:41:39.560
understand the
concept of editing

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:44.120
because that person
thought that I did not

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:47.480
have the right shots,
which I knew I did.

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:49.200
Remember, I used
to be an editor.

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:51.120
But I'm not going to
lead with, I'm an editor,

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:52.160
I know I'm right.

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:52.960
No.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:59.000
I had to cut the scene while
filming, which DGA-wise,

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:01.400
you don't have to do.

00:42:01.400 --> 00:42:02.520
I'm protected.

00:42:02.520 --> 00:42:06.680
But I chose to cut the scene as
a rough while I was shooting,

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:08.840
mind you.

00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:11.080
And meanwhile, I should
cook for everybody--

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:12.440
Not an abundance of time to--

00:42:12.440 --> 00:42:12.920
Right?

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:13.520
Yeah.

00:42:13.520 --> 00:42:16.440
And [LAUGHS] I
should do all that.

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:19.480
And I sent the scene
in to the studio.

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:22.200
And then they called
apologizing to me.

00:42:22.200 --> 00:42:25.880
But I'm a solution
oriented person,

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:27.680
so it's about
finding a solution.

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:32.120
I can't get bogged
down in why, who, why

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:33.720
are they feeling that way.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:36.000
It just will not
help the situation.

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:38.960
And it is about kind of
a focus and a mentality

00:42:38.960 --> 00:42:41.320
that I love talking
about with filmmakers

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:43.000
because that's half the battle.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:44.600
Everybody is talented.

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:47.600
But it's, how are you dealing
with the interpersonal

00:42:47.600 --> 00:42:51.280
back and forth with people?

00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:53.120
So I mean, I have
a million stories.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:54.920
That's just a nice sample.

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:59.000
I've had way worse stories that
we do not want to get into.

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:00.320
But I'm good for it.

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:05.480
But it's an example of how
I have to be a partner.

00:43:05.480 --> 00:43:07.040
We have a couple more here.

00:43:07.040 --> 00:43:09.920
And you actually kind of touched
on it, too, a little bit.

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:11.360
What are the first
questions you're

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:13.720
asking in that first
moment with the script?

00:43:13.720 --> 00:43:15.080
Which I always
think is so great.

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:16.920
It's after the director
is given a script,

00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:18.560
or maybe after they've
written a script.

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:20.680
It's that first moment,
from that moment

00:43:20.680 --> 00:43:23.800
when they switch off the writer
brain to when they're then

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:25.000
into the director.

00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:26.480
What are those first questions?

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:29.120
What are those first
sort of, I would say,

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:31.040
rabbit holes you're
going down to to find

00:43:31.040 --> 00:43:33.920
the visual side of the story?

00:43:33.920 --> 00:43:36.280
meaning I've already
accepted the script.

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:36.800
Yes.

00:43:36.800 --> 00:43:38.880
Already accepted the
script, and now you're

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:42.120
like, what is that first
thinking about the characters,

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:42.600
the shot?

00:43:42.600 --> 00:43:44.720
Like, what is those
first moments with it?

00:43:44.720 --> 00:43:47.280
Well, each script is different.

00:43:47.280 --> 00:43:50.720
I think the script and the
story and the characters

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:52.440
will tell you what it is.

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:56.080
Like, sometimes
I'm reading a scene

00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:58.560
and I go, oh, I get the style.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:02.600
This is going to be
handheld with kind

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:05.920
of like a voyeuristic
camera angle.

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:11.560
I start seeing those kind
of framings and energy

00:44:11.560 --> 00:44:17.080
in terms of how to
capture the characters.

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:17.960
I think about that.

00:44:17.960 --> 00:44:22.280
I also think about who's going
to play these characters.

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:25.200
I also want to make sure
that it all matters,

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:27.680
and that the characters
are super authentic,

00:44:27.680 --> 00:44:33.080
and that we can
understand the story

00:44:33.080 --> 00:44:34.560
from a different point of view.

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:37.320
So I mean, my process can vary.

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:40.000
But I definitely pull images.

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:42.560
I'll just scrounge around for
images from, like, photography

00:44:42.560 --> 00:44:43.080
books.

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:46.880
I'm like, oh, this made me
think of like Shibuya and Tokyo

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:48.040
when I was there.

00:44:48.040 --> 00:44:49.960
Let me pull this--
the crowdedness.

00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:54.400
Like, I'm a little left of field
with the way I pull my images.

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:57.880
And then I start creating
these color palettes.

00:44:57.880 --> 00:45:01.000
I'm very intent on what
the vibe is going to be.

00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:04.040
And so it's not
all over the place.

00:45:04.040 --> 00:45:11.680
And then, once I know what it
is, I land on it, so to speak.

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:12.760
I'll look for that.

00:45:12.760 --> 00:45:14.840
I'm going to go a little
off script with this one.

00:45:14.840 --> 00:45:16.360
But you mentioned
that in addition

00:45:16.360 --> 00:45:18.640
to reading these
photography books and all

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:20.640
of that in your research,
you mentioned that you

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:22.080
watch a lot of foreign films.

00:45:22.080 --> 00:45:24.120
I'm just going to, because
I'm curious as to what

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:25.360
I need to be watching next.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:27.120
What are you watching?

00:45:27.120 --> 00:45:29.560
What should I go pick up
if I maybe haven't seen it,

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:31.200
which I'm always excited to do.

00:45:31.200 --> 00:45:36.120
Well, [LAUGHS] the foreign
films I watch are older movies.

00:45:36.120 --> 00:45:40.120
So it's like
[SPEAKING FRENCH] which

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:41.440
is Last Year at Marienbad.

00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:43.760
[SPEAKING FRENCH] is one
of my favorite films.

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:47.920
Another great film that
I love the energy of it

00:45:47.920 --> 00:45:52.680
is Godard's Contempt,
[SPEAKING FRENCH]..

00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:56.320
There's Cache, which is
more of a modern thing.

00:45:56.320 --> 00:46:00.720
There's [SPEAKING FRENCH]
Cleo from 5 to 7.

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:03.800
Like, I watch a lot
of the classics.

00:46:03.800 --> 00:46:07.760
Also, I've been watching
recently some horror.

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:10.360
There's a very scary
movie from France

00:46:10.360 --> 00:46:14.680
called Martyrs, which is really
very interesting camerawork

00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:15.640
and stuff that I study.

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:18.640
Like, I'll study different
things for different reasons--

00:46:18.640 --> 00:46:25.440
lighting exposures
and lenses and stuff.

00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:28.040
And then, of course, I'm
on Criterion all the time

00:46:28.040 --> 00:46:31.240
and seeing what's there.

00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:35.640
I watch a lot of
African films, too.

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:40.760
And I see what's
happening back home.

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:43.920
So that matters to me, too.

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:44.720
I love that.

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:46.520
I actually just
recently finally got

00:46:46.520 --> 00:46:49.680
to see Ganja &amp; Hess for the
first time, which I think

00:46:49.680 --> 00:46:50.840
is just an incredible film.

00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:53.520
And since you mentioned
French filmmaker and horror,

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:55.040
I'm just going to
go and say, if you

00:46:55.040 --> 00:46:58.640
haven't seen Julia Decournau's
Raw, I live for that movie.

00:46:58.640 --> 00:47:02.120
It is one of the most twisted
horror coming of age story

00:47:02.120 --> 00:47:02.800
you'll ever see.

00:47:02.800 --> 00:47:03.400
I live for it.

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:03.920
Oh, my God.

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:06.040
So [LAUGHS] I know that
was a bit of a detour.

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:07.280
But hey, we're at the Academy.

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:08.960
It's all about recommendations.

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:10.160
Totally.

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:13.520
The two words that will
make any film fan light up

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:14.960
is Criterion Collection.

00:47:14.960 --> 00:47:16.240
[LAUGHING]

00:47:16.240 --> 00:47:16.840
I know.

00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:18.960
I mean, that's what
I do on my off time.

00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:20.280
[LAUGHS]

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:21.040
Exactly.

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:23.320
Well, I just have to say this.

00:47:23.320 --> 00:47:26.840
It was a real joy to
take a small peek back

00:47:26.840 --> 00:47:28.960
at your incredible
filmography and all the work

00:47:28.960 --> 00:47:29.680
that you've done.

00:47:29.680 --> 00:47:31.040
I felt that I learned something.

00:47:31.040 --> 00:47:32.520
I everyone listening to this.

00:47:32.520 --> 00:47:35.160
So just thank you so
much for your time.

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:36.680
And I also want to
thank the Academy

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:39.520
for inviting us here to do this
because it was really a treat.

00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:42.440
And I think it's just fun to
talk movies with movie people.

00:47:42.440 --> 00:47:44.000
So thank you so much.

00:47:44.000 --> 00:47:45.160
Thank you so much.

00:47:45.160 --> 00:47:46.000
It was fun.

